"# Default rigor\n\nBefore opening or updating a pull request: bring it up to date with the default branch and resolve any overlap yourself; read the whole diff once against the user's words and your self-brief, and revert or name every change the words did not ask for; update every document the change makes stale; and validate the change yourself, evidence first. Demonstrate the intent end-to-end: list the concrete actions an end user would take and their expected results; gather evidence for each: product-level artifacts that directly show the behavior working, such as screenshots, recordings, rendered UI, CLI transcripts, API responses, persisted state, or logs. Use as many artifacts as it takes, pictures included for a visible surface. Unit tests passing, generic pass/fail output, and reading the code are not evidence on their own. Keep every artifact in your task's evidence folder, never in the working copy or a commit. Verified states what each artifact showed, or what stopped you gathering it. A picture the repository's own instructions keep with the change, such as a release note's hero picture, is part of the change, not evidence: commit it where they say. Show image artifacts on the merge card by their path in the evidence folder. Your brief names visible views and states. Before editing, photograph each existing view into evidence; a captain-attached or reproduction picture serves as Before. Photograph each noticeable view or state afterward, matching Before's window size, theme, data and crop; retake after later edits. When a still cannot show a drag, keyboard journey, motion, progress or error recovery, say why in the brief; record the same action before and after editing at real speed through the isolated test launcher. A visual change keeps its pictures. Run the smallest set of existing tests that would catch a break in what an end user experiences, end-to-end tests covering the changed surfaces included, and re-run them after any rebase that brings in overlapping changes. Where end-to-end tests read a surface through shared helpers, the tests covering it are those helpers' callers, and a change to what a helper reads is fixed once in the helper, never in copies across the tests. If no existing test produces the evidence, write or improve a test so one does; if automated testing cannot, perform manual verification steps and record them. During fixes, avoid unrequested rewrites of AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md; still review explicit changes to them, resolve conflicts and update instructions the change actually makes stale. Your report carries five headings in this order - Intent, What changed, Verified, Risk, Not done. Verified accounts for the complete validation: exact commands and tests run, manual checks, and the smallest evidence that the intent is met. Risk is low, medium or high with one sentence. A test whose only evidence is that source text contains or omits a string proves nothing; exercise an interface and assert behavior.\n\nWrite a code comment only as a decision record: what the code cannot show, such as why it is this way, an invariant, an order that matters, a failure it prevents, a unit, or a library's sharp edge and the way around it. Never write a tautology (what the name, signature or next lines already say, in tests too), an excuse (that a shortcut is fine, such as a TODO, a swallowed error, a cast, a fixed sleep or copied logic; fix the shortcut, or file a follow-up when the fix lies outside your task) or a stale comment (no longer so: a removed name, past behavior, a plan, or the review that asked for the code, which belongs in the commit message). Ignore how densely nearby code is commented. Before your report, fix or delete each comment your change made untrue and each it adds or edits that fails these words; keep one you cannot judge. The project's own rule on comments wins.\n"
